{
  "action": "tool",
  "tool": "retrieve_history",
  "args": {
    "research_concepts": [
      "defensive impact",
      "opponent performance change",
      "halftime adjustments",
      "team traits",
      "reliability of metrics"
    ],
    "methodological_questions": [
      "how to measure performance change across game halves (H2-H1)?",
      "how to isolate a defense's specific effect on opponent performance?",
      "how to quantify the reliability of a team-level metric?",
      "what are common confounders in studies of in-game performance dynamics or defensive impact?"
    ],
    "query": "Is a defense's effect on opponents' H2-H1 performance change a reliable team trait? Historical studies on defensive impact, halftime adjustments, and metric reliability in basketball."
  }
}

TOOL_RESULT retrieve_history:
{"results": [], "n_results": 0}

Stage 2. Using the retrieval result above (follow-up tools are allowed within the tool budget), complete steps 4 to 9
and reply with the final action. Output schema (all fields required):
{
 "research_concepts": [
  "concept"
 ],
 "methodological_questions": [
  "question"
 ],
 "historical_evidence": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a record returned by a tool",
   "status_as_recorded": "outcome status exactly as stated in the record (supported | rejected | inconclusive | untestable | artifact | not a hypothesis test | no result recorded)",
   "finding": "what the record states, in its own terms"
  }
 ],
 "failed_approaches": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a returned record",
   "what_failed": "approach",
   "why": "failure mechanism or limitation as recorded"
  }
 ],
 "evidence_establishes": [
  "what the retrieved evidence establishes for this question"
 ],
 "evidence_does_not_establish": [
  "what it leaves open or cannot show"
 ],
 "own_inferences": [
  "your inference, not stated in any record"
 ],
 "uncertainties": [
  "uncertainty or missing information"
 ],
 "historical_effect": {
  "changed_decision": "yes | no | no_historical_evidence",
  "explanation": "whether and how the historical evidence changed the decision"
 },
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": "number in [0,1]: probability the question can be answered with the stated data at useful power",
  "blocking_constraints": [
   {
    "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
    "text": "string"
   }
  ]
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "yes | partly | no | unknown",
  "cited": [
   "identifiers of prior work relied on"
  ],
  "summary": "string"
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
   "text": "string"
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": "probability",
  "rejected": "probability",
  "untestable": "probability",
  "artifact": "probability",
  "uncertain": "probability"
 },
 "decision": "proceed | proceed_with_changes | do_not_proceed",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "string",
  "unit": "string",
  "sample": "string",
  "power": "string",
  "controls": "string",
  "falsification": "string",
  "multiplicity": "string",
  "decision_rule": "string",
  "data_version": "string"
 }
}
Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{
 "research_concepts": [
  "defensive impact",
  "opponent performance change",
  "halftime adjustments",
  "team traits",
  "reliability of metrics"
 ],
 "methodological_questions": [
  "how to measure performance change across game halves (H2-H1)?",
  "how to isolate a defense's specific effect on opponent performance?",
  "how to quantify the reliability of a team-level metric?",
  "what are common confounders in studies of in-game performance dynamics or defensive impact?"
 ],
 "historical_evidence": [],
 "failed_approaches": [],
 "evidence_establishes": [],
 "evidence_does_not_establish": [
  "No historical evidence was available to establish anything regarding a defense's effect on opponents' H2-H1 change as a reliable team trait."
 ],
 "own_inferences": [
  "Measuring H2-H1 performance change is feasible with the available play-by-play data.",
  "Isolating a defense's specific effect from other factors (e.g., opponent's own adjustments, shot variance, game state) will be the primary methodological challenge.",
  "Assessing 'reliability' as a 'team trait' implies consistency of this effect across games and potentially across seasons for a given team."
 ],
 "uncertainties": [
  "The magnitude of a defense's effect on H2-H1 opponent performance change is unknown.",
  "The inherent variability and noise in H2-H1 performance changes, making it difficult to detect a stable 'trait'.",
  "The specific mechanisms by which defenses might induce H2-H1 changes (e.g., forcing specific shot types, reducing efficiency, disrupting flow) are not defined in the question."
 ],
 "historical_effect": {
  "changed_decision": "no_historical_evidence",
  "explanation": "No historical evidence was available, so it did not change the decision."
 },
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": 0.6,
  "blocking_constraints": []
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "no",
  "cited": [],
  "summary": "No prior work on this specific question was found in the historical records."
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "CONFOUNDING",
   "text": "Opponent's own halftime adjustments, natural regression to the mean for outlier H1 performances, game state (e.g., score differential, home/away), player fatigue, substitution patterns, and officiating can all confound the observed H2-H1 change and make it difficult to isolate the defense's specific, intentional effect."
  },
  {
   "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
   "text": "While 120 team-seasons are available, detecting a subtle 'reliable team trait' from noisy H2-H1 performance differentials, especially after controlling for numerous confounders, might require a larger sample or a very strong effect size to achieve sufficient statistical power."
  },
  {
   "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
   "text": "The H2-H1 change in opponent performance might itself be a highly variable and noisy metric, making it difficult to establish its 'reliability' as a team trait, rather than just random game-to-game variance."
  },
  {
   "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
   "text": "Specific defensive strategies or opponent types might not have enough occurrences within a team-season to derive a stable and reliable H2-H1 effect."
  },
  {
   "code": "DRIFT_NONSTATIONARITY",
   "text": "Team rosters, coaching staff, and league-wide rules/trends can change across seasons, potentially making a 'team trait' derived from H2-H1 changes non-stationary and challenging to assess for reliability over multiple seasons."
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": 0.1,
  "rejected": 0.2,
  "untestable": 0.3,
  "artifact": 0.1,
  "uncertain": 0.3
 },
 "decision": "proceed_with_changes",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "The estimand is the intra-class correlation (ICC) or season-to-season correlation of a team's average defensive H2-H1 suppression effect on opponent offensive efficiency (e.g., points per possession, effective field goal percentage), after controlling for opponent's H1 performance, game context, and other confounding factors. The 'suppression effect' would be estimated as the residual H2-H1 change in opponent offensive efficiency that is attributable to the defense, beyond what is explained by other factors.",
  "unit": "The primary unit of analysis for establishing reliability is the team-season. Within each team-season, individual games (H2 relative to H1) will be used to calculate the average H2-H1 effect.",
  "sample": "All NBA regular season and playoff games from the 2022-23, 2023-24, 2024-25, and 2025-26 seasons (4 full seasons + partial 2025-26). This provides approximately 120 unique team-seasons for analysis.",
  "power": "A power analysis will be conducted using simulations to determine the minimum detectable effect size for the ICC or correlation, given the expected variance in H2-H1 performance and the number of games per team-season. This will inform whether a meaningful effect can be reliably detected with the available data.",
  "controls": "A mixed-effects regression model will be used to estimate the H2-H1 change in opponent offensive efficiency (e.g., points per possession, eFG%) for each game, with random intercepts for defensive team-season. Fixed effects will include: opponent's H1 offensive efficiency, game score differential at halftime, home/away status, opponent's overall team strength (e.g., pre-game betting line or season-long offensive rating), game pace, and interaction terms where appropriate. Player-level controls (e.g., aggregate minutes played by key players in H1 vs H2) should also be considered if feasible.",
  "falsification": "The null hypothesis is that the observed H2-H1 defensive suppression effect is not significantly more consistent across games or seasons for a given team than expected by random chance. This can be falsified if the calculated ICC or season-to-season correlation for the defensive H2-H1 effect is statistically significant (p < 0.05) and of a practically meaningful magnitude, indicating a reliable team trait. A permutation test could also be used to compare the observed reliability against a distribution of random assignments.",
  "multiplicity": "If multiple metrics of offensive efficiency (e.g., points per possession, eFG%, turnover rate) are used to define 'opponent performance', a Bonferroni correction or False Discovery Rate (FDR) adjustment will be applied to the p-values to account for multiple comparisons.",
  "decision_rule": "The research question will be considered 'supported' if the estimated reliability metric (ICC or season-to-season correlation) for the defensive H2-H1 suppression effect is statistically significant (p < 0.05) and exceeds a pre-specified threshold for practical significance (e.g., ICC > 0.3). Otherwise, it will be considered 'rejected' if adequately powered, or 'uncertain' if statistical power is insufficient or results are inconclusive.",
  "data_version": "NBA 2022-23 regular season and playoffs, NBA 2023-24 regular season and playoffs, NBA 2024-25 regular season and playoffs, NBA 2025-26 regular season (complete from 2026-04-13), NBA 2025-26 playoffs (complete from 2026-06-21)."
 }
}

